==English==
{{wp|<dab!>}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|natuer||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:nature|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵenh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|nature}}, {{m|enm|natur}}, from {{der|en|fro|nature}}, from {{der|en|la|nātūra||birth, origin, natural constitution or quality}}, future participle from perfect passive participle {{m|la|natus|(g)natus|born}}, from [[deponent]] verb {{m|la|nasci|(g)nasci|to be born, originate}} + future participle suffix {{m|en|-urus}}.

Displaced native {{ncog|enm|erd|t=character, nature, disposition}} from {{ncog|ang|eard}} (compare {{ncog|de|Art|t=nature, character, kind, type}}); and {{ncog|enm|kynde|t=character, disposition, nature}} from {{ncog|ang|ġecynd}}. More at {{l|en|kind}}. Also displaced native {{noncog|ang|hād||nature, manner}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|nā′-chə(r)}} {{IPA|en|/ˈneɪ̯.tʃə(ɹ)/}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈneɪ̯.tʃə/|a=RP}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈneɪ̯.t͡ʃəɹ/|a=GenAm}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈnɛː.tʃɐ/|a=Northern England}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈnæɪ̯.tʃə/|[ˈnæe̯tʃɐ]|[ˈnae̯tʃɐ]|a=AU,NZ}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-nature.ogg|a=US}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-nature.ogg|a=UK}}
** {{rhymes|en|eɪtʃə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|na|ture}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable|often|_|capitalized}} The [[way]] [[things]] [[are]], the [[totality]] of [[all]] [[thing]]s in the [[physical]] [[universe]] and [[their]] [[order]], [[especially]] the [[physical]] [[world]] [[in contrast]] to [[spiritual]] [[realm]]s and [[flora]] and [[fauna]] as [[distinct]] from [[human]] [[convention]]s, [[art]], and [[technology]].
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=8|passage=I oft admire<br>How '''Nature''', wise and frugal, could commit<br>Such disproportions.|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1808|author=w:Dugald Stewart|title=Elements of the Philosophy of the Human Mind|url=https://archive.org/details/b29319663/page/314/mode/2up?q=train|pages=315–6
|passage=In the works of '''nature''' we find, in many instances, beauty and sublimity involved among circumstances, which are either indifferent, or which obstruct the general effect: and it is only by a train of experiments, that we can separate those circumstances from the rest... Accordingly, the inexperienced artist, when he copies '''nature''', will copy her servilely... and the beauties of his performances will be encumbered with a number of superfluous or disagreeable concomitants. Experience and observation alone can enable him to make this determination: to exhibit the principles of beauty pure and unadulterated, and to form a creation of his own, more faultless, than ever fell under the observation of his senses.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1816|author={{w|Matthew Jouett|Matthew Harris Jouett}}|title=Notes... on Painting with {{w|Gilbert Stuart|Gilbert Stuart Esqr}}
|passage=Most persons in striving after effect lose the likeness when they should go together to produce a good effect you must copy '''Nature''': leave '''Nature''' for an imaginary effect & you lose all. '''Nature''' as '''Nature''' cannot be exceeded, and as your object it [is] to copy '''Nature''' [[twere]] the [[height|hight]] of folly to look at any thing else to produce that copy.}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=6|passage='''Nature''' has caprices which art cannot imitate.}}
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne Scarlet Letter|page=[https://archive.org/details/scarletletterrom01hawt/page/186/mode/2up 186–7]|text=Nothing was more common, in those days, than to interpret all meteoric appearances, and other natural phenomena, that occurred with less regularity than the rise and set of sun and moon, as so many revelations from a supernatural source... But what shall we say, when an individual discovers a revelation, addressed to himself alone, on the same vast sheet of record! In such a case, it could only be the symptom of a highly disordered mental state, when a man, rendered morbidly self-contemplative by long, intense, and secret pain, had extended his egotism over the whole expanse of '''nature''', until the firmament itself should appear no more than a fitting page for his soul's history and fate.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1891|author=w:Oscar Wilde|title=w:The Decay of Lying
|passage='''Nature''' has good intentions, of course, but, as {{w|Aristotle}} once said, she cannot carry them out. When I look at a landscape I cannot help seeing all its defects.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=w:Jude the Obscure|page=15
|passage='''Nature'''’s logic was too horrid for him to care for.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|translator=w:Constance Garnett|author=w:Fyodor Dostoevsky|chapter=w:Notes from Underground|title=White Nights and Other Stories|url=https://archive.org/details/whitenightsother00dostiala/page/58/mode/2up|pages=58–9
|passage=...they will shout at you, it is no use protesting: it is a case of twice two makes four! '''Nature''' does not ask your permission, she has nothing to do with your wishes, and whether you like her laws or dislike them, you are bound to accept her as she is, and consequently all her conclusions. A wall, you see, is a wall... Merciful Heavens! but what do I care for the laws of '''nature''' and arithmetic, when, for some reason I dislike those laws and the fact that twice two makes four? Of course I cannot break through the wall by battering my head against it if I really have not the strength to knock it down, but I am not going to be reconciled to it simply because it is a stone wall and I have not the strength.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1928|author=Christopher Dawson|title=The Age of the Gods|page=49
|passage=Man was entirely at the mercy of '''nature'''—a mere scavenger who eked out a miserable existence as a food-gatherer and an eater of shell-fish.}}
#* '''2006 Oct. 1''', {{w|Dennis Lehane}}, "{{w|Refugees (The Wire)|Refugees}}", ''{{w|The Wire}}'', 00:34:06:
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Alisa Luxenberg|chapter=Printing Plants: The Technology of '''Nature''' Printing in Eighteenth-Century Spain|title=Art, Technology, and '''Nature'''|page=140
|passage={{w|Casimiro Gómez Ortega|Gómez Ortega}}... explicitly ordered them to study only fresh plants, [[in situ]], to draw every part, and 'to copy '''nature''' exactly without presuming to correct it or decorate it as some draughtsmen are used to doing, adding colours and ornaments drawn from their imagination'.}}
#* '''2017 Sept. 8''', {{w|Michael Grunwald}}, "[https://www.politico.com/magazine/story/2017/09/08/hurricane-irma-florida-215586/ A Requiem for Florida]" in ''Politico Magazine'':
#*: As {{w|Hurricane Irma}} prepares to strike, it's worth remembering that [[Mother Nature|Mother '''Nature''']] never intended us to live here.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Olof G. Lidin|title=From Taoism to Einstein|page=196
|passage=The [[tao]] of {{w|Lao Tzu}} was a cosmic [[tao]], inner and unwritten, a [[tao]] of '''Nature''', while the [[tao]] of {{w|Confucius}} was moral and written.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Nature''' doesn't lie.}}
#: {{ux|en|The [[laws of nature|laws of '''nature''']] are written in the language of [[mathematics]].}}
#: {{ux|en|Tectonic activity is part of '''nature''', so there's no way to stop earthquakes.}}
# {{senseid|en|innate qualities}}The [[particular]] [[way]] [[someone]] or [[something]] [[is]], [[especially]]
## The [[essential]] or [[innate]] [[characteristics]] of a [[person]] or [[thing]] which will [[always]] [[tend]] to [[manifest]], [[especially]] [[in contrast]] to [[specific]] [[context]]s, [[reason]], [[religious]] [[duty]], [[upbringing]], and [[personal]] [[pretense]] or [[effort]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|Troilus and Cressida|III|iii|text=''{{w|Odysseus|Vliss.}}'': ... One touch of '''nature''' makes the whole world kin,<br>That all with one consent praise [[newborn|new-borne]] [[gaud]]es,<br>Though they are made and moulded of things past,<br>And [[go]]e to dust, that is a little [[gilt|guilt]],<br>More laud then [[gilt|guilt]] [[over|ore]]-dusted.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|I|v|text=''Lady.'' ...Glamys thou art, and Cawdor, and shalt be<br>What thou art promis'd: yet [[do]]e I [[fear]]e thy '''Nature''',<br>It is too full [[of|o']][[the|th']] [[milk|Milke]] of [[human]]e [[kindness]]e,<br>To catch the [[nearest|neerest]] way.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1641|author=David Fergusson|title=Scottish proverbs|section=D4
|passage='''Nature''' passes [[nurture|norture]].}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1709|author=Robert Steele|journal=Tatler|issue=93
|passage=Men may change their Climate, but they cannot their '''Nature'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1834|author=Criminal Law Commission|pageurl=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=-tRbAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA2-PA21|page=21|title=First Report... on Criminal Law
|passage=Domestic animals of a base '''nature''' and not fit for food, are not the subjects of theft. This rule includes dogs and cats.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author=w:Edward Bulwer-Lytton|title=Harold|volume=III|page=375
|passage=His own [[better nature|better '''nature''']] which... was magnanimous and heroic, moved and won him.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1874|author=John Henry Blunt|title=Dictionary of Sects...|page=332
|passage=The {{w|Monophysite}}s held that the two '''Natures''' were so united, that although the 'One {{w|Christ}}' was partly Human and partly Divine, His two '''Natures''' became by their union only one '''Nature'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author=w:Horatio Alger Jr.|title=s:Mark the Match Boy|section=[[s:Mark the Match Boy/Chapter 16|Ch. 16]]
|passage=Mark hardly knew whether to believe this or not. He already began to suspect that Roswell was something of a humbug, and though it was not in his '''nature''' to form a causeless dislike, he certainly did not feel disposed to like Roswell.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1874|author=Francis Galton|title=English Men of Science|page=12
|passage=The phrase ‘'''nature''' and [[nurture]]’ is a convenient jingle of words, for it separates under two distinct heads the innumerable elements of which personality is composed.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=w:Herman Cyril McNeile|title={{w|Bulldog Drummond (novel)|Bulldog Drummond}}|chapter=1
|passage=Being by '''nature''' of a cheerful disposition, the symptom did not surprise his servant, late private of the same famous regiment, who was laying breakfast in an adjoining room.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=Richard Henry Tawney|title=Religion and the Rise of Capitalism|page=20
|passage=The contrast between '''nature''' and [[grace]], between human appetites and interests and religion, is not absolute, but relative.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1961|author=Barry Crump|title=Hang on a Minute Mate|page=147
|passage=Couples bitching at each other is [[human nature|human '''nature''']].}}
##* '''2006 Oct. 1''', {{w|Dennis Lehane}}, "{{w|Refugees (The Wire)|Refugees}}", ''{{w|The Wire}}'', 00:34:06:
##: {{ux|en|It's not in my '''nature''' to steal.}}
##: {{ux|en|You can't help feeling that way. It's [[human nature|human '''nature''']].}}
##: {{ux|en|[[power corrupts, absolute power corrupts absolutely|Power corrupts]]. That's just the [[nature of the beast|'''nature''' of the beast]].}}
## The [[distinguish]]ing [[characteristic]] of a person or thing, understood as its general class, sort, type, etc.
##* '''1626 July 12''', {{w|Charles I of England|Charles I}}, Instructions:
##* {{RQ:Dryden Fables|Preface
|passage=A dispute of this '''nature''' caused mischief.}}
##* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=II|text=Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers, of errand not wholly obvious to their fellows, yet of such sort as to call into query alike the '''nature''' of their errand and their own relations.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1949|author=w:George Orwell|title=w:Nineteen Eighty-Four|page=56
|passage=And yet, though you could not actually hear what the man was saying, you could not be in any doubt about its general '''nature'''.}}
##*: The extent and '''nature''' of {{w|Bach}}'s influence on {{w|Haydn}} is now due for further reassessment.
##: {{ux|en|What was the '''nature''' of your relationship with the deceased?}}
##: {{ux|en|The best medium might be petroleum, liquified gas, or something of that '''nature'''.}}
## {{lb|en|UK|_|military|obsolete}} {{synonym of|en|caliber}}: the [[class]] of a [[gun]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1828|author=James Morton Spearman|title=The British Gunner|page=130
|passage=...One Hundred of each '''Nature''' of Case-Shot...}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1879|author=w:War Office|title=Manual of Siege and Garrison Artillery Exercises|pageurl=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=-C8BAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA37|page=37
|passage=[[breechloader|B.L.]] cartridges have lubricators choked inside the cartridges of 40-[[pr.]] and lower '''natures'''.}}
# The [[vital]] [[function]]s or [[strength]] of [[someone]] or [[something]], [[especially]] {{lb|en|now|_|dialect}} as [[requiring]] [[nourishment]] or [[careful]] [[maintenance]] or {{lb|en|medicine}} as a [[force]] of [[regeneration]] without [[special]] [[treatment]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1592|author=William West|title=Symbolaeography|section=Pt. I, §102b
|passage=Any such [[corrosive|corrasiue]], [[sharp]]e or eager medicine... as the said H. [[shall|shal]] think his '''nature''' is [[unable|vnable]] to suffer...}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet Q1-2|version=Q2|act=I|scene=iii|page=19|passage=For '''nature''' creſſant does not grovve alone / In thevvs and bulkes, but as this temple vvaxes, / The invvard ſervice of the minde and ſoule / Grovves vvide vvithal, {{...}}|translation=For a '''human being's vital functions''', increasing, do not grow alone / In physical development and bulk, but as this "temple" [''i.e.'', the body] waxes, / The inward operation of the mind and soul / Grows wide with them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1807|author=w:Zebulon Pike|title=An Account of Expeditions to the Source of the Mississippi...|volume=II|page=182
|passage=I returned hungry... and had only snow to supply the calls of '''nature'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1820|author=Thomas Tredgold|title=Elementary Principles of Carpentry|page=165
|passage=The timber... is found to be brittle and effete; or, to use the workman's expression, 'its '''nature''' is gone'.}}
#* {{RQ:Borrow Bible in Spain|passage=The prison allowance will not support '''nature'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895|author=T. Pinnock|title=Tom Brown's Black Country Annual...
|passage=My iron’s just [[coming|comin’]] to '''natur’'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1984|author={{w|W. N. Herbert|William N. Herbert}}|title=Sterts & Stobies|page=30
|passage=''Hungry-groond'', ground credited to be so much enchanted that a person passing over it would faint if they did not use something to support '''nature'''.}}
# A [[requirement]] or [[powerful]] [[impulse]] of the [[body]]'s [[physical]] [[form]], [[especially]]
## The [[need]] to [[urinate]] and [[defecate]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1701|author=William Wotton|title=The History of Rome|page=328
|passage=He withdrew from the Company to ease '''Nature'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1965|author=Wole Soyinka|title=Road|page=26
|passage=The women tell you to stop because they's feeling the call of '''nature'''. If you don't stop they pee in your lorry.}}
##: {{ux|en|I hear the [[call of nature|call of '''nature''']].}}
## {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|AAVE}} [[sexual|Sexual]] [[desire]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1823|author=w:Lord Byron|title={{w|Don Juan (poem)|Don Juan}}|sectionurl=https://petercochran.files.wordpress.com/2009/03/don_juan_canto_15.pdf|section=Draft, Canto XV, St. xlix & lii
|passage=She marvelled "What he saw in such a baby<br>"As that prim, silent, cold Aurora Raby?"<br>...''Why'' Adeline had this slight prejudice<br>...For me appears a question far too nice,<br>Since Adeline was liberal by Nature;<br>But '''Nature'''’s '''Nature''', and has more caprices<br>Than I have time, or will to take to pieces...}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1941|author=William Alexander Percy|title=Lanterns on the Levee|page=305
|passage=He had placed a spell on her by means of a [[conjure|cunjer]] bag... Its effect was to rob her of [[connubial]] allure—in her words, ‘it stole her '''nature'''’.}}
##* '''2006 Oct. 1''', {{w|Dennis Lehane}}, "{{w|Refugees (The Wire)|Refugees}}", ''{{w|The Wire}}'', 00:34:06:
## {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|UK|_|regional|&|AAVE}} [[spontaneous|Spontaneous]] [[love]], [[affection]], or [[reverence]], [[especially]] between [[parent]] and [[child]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|I|v|text=''Lady.'' ... Come you Spirits,<br>That tend on [[mortal]]l thoughts, [[unsex|vnsex]] me here,<br>...make thick my blood,<br>Stop [[up|vp]] [[the|th']][[access]]e, and passage to Remorse,<br>That no compunctious visitings of '''Nature'''<br>Shake my fell purpose...}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1712|author=w:Alexander Pope|chapterurl=https://www.bartleby.com/203/4.html|chapter=The First Book of Statius's Thebais|title=Miscellaneous Poems and Translations|page=25
|passage=Have we not seen (the blood of {{w|Laius}} shed)<br>The [[murdering|murd'ring]] son ascend his parent's bed,<br>[[through|Thro']] violated '''Nature''' force his way,<br>And stain the sacred womb where once he lay?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220122074712/https://www.bartleby.com/203/4.html|archivedate=22 January 2022}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=John Cleland|title=Memoirs of a Woman of Pleasure|volume=I|page=136
|passage=She had no '''nature''', nor indeed any passion but that of money.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|author=w:Robinson Jeffers|chapter=Thurso's Landing|title=Selected Poetry|url=https://archive.org/details/selectedpoertyof029969mbp/page/312/mode/2up|page=312
|passage=...I could bear much. I'd not move nor scream<br>While you wrote the red stripes:<br>But there's no '''nature''' in you...}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} A [[product]] of the [[body]]'s [[physical]] [[form]], [[especially]] [[semen]] and [[vaginal fluid]]s, [[menstrual fluid]], and {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[feces]].
#* {{c.|1938}}, spell cited in Harry Middleton Hyatt, ''Hoodoo Conjuration Witchcraft Rootwork'', Vol. I, p. 534:
# {{lb|en|now|_|rare}} A [[part]] of the [[body]]'s [[physical]] [[form]], [[especially]] {{lb|en|obsolete}} the [[female]] [[genitalia]].

====Usage notes====
* In its primary sense as the material world, its inhabitants, and their order, ''nature'' is frequently [[personify|personified]] in English conversation and literature, primarily as a cold and indifferent entity or as a wise and loving nurturer (see {{m|en|Mother Nature}}). In its sense as the essential characteristics of humanity, man's present ''nature'' is usually taken in Christian thought as debased by {{l|en|original sin}} or inherent frailty but amenable to purification through {{l|en|grace}}; English consideration of {{l|en|human nature}} frequently continues to maintain a similar focus on resigned acceptance of its failings and distinctions between better/higher and worse/lower ''natures''.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|innate characteristics}} See [[Thesaurus:essence]]

